WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.241
Detection engineering often begins by asking which behaviors matter most.

2
00:00:04.241 --> 00:00:12.981
Threat intelligence, adversary tactics, organizational priorities, and historical incidents can all inform where detection effort should be spent.

3
00:00:12.981 --> 00:00:17.002
The objective is to translate threat knowledge into observable signals.

4
00:00:17.002 --> 00:00:25.673
Instead of trying to monitor everything equally, teams identify behaviors that are relevant to their systems, data, users, and risk profile.

5
00:00:25.673 --> 00:00:34.134
A strong detection idea includes a hypothesis, required telemetry, expected behavior, investigation value, and assumptions.

6
00:00:34.134 --> 00:00:39.921
It should be clear why the behavior matters and what an analyst is expected to do when the signal appears.

7
00:00:39.921 --> 00:00:43.752
Risk-informed detection work helps teams make practical tradeoffs.

8
00:00:43.752 --> 00:00:54.109
Some detections may be high priority because they align to likely threats, protect critical services, or address lessons learned from recent operational experience.
